 We have a polynomial $f(x)=x^4+2ax^3+(2a-2)x^2+(-4a+3)x-2.$ The derivative of $f$ is
\begin{align*}f'(x)&=4x^3+6ax^2+2(2a-2)x-4a+3\\ &\hspace{2.17793em}=2(2x^3+3ax^2+2)a-2(2a-1)x-2(2a-1)\\&\hspace{2.17793em}=\big(2x+(2a-1)\big)(x-1)^2\end{align*}
Thus $f$ has at most a double root of $1$ and hence it cannot have all real (multiple) roots if it has any double root. Thus $f$ must have four distinct real roots iff $f$ has no double root.  This happens iff $f\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)>0\Rightarrow\frac{1}{4}<2a-1.$ Thus $a>\frac{5}{8}.$</think> <answer> $\boxed{\frac 38}$</answer>

